Tasks

1. Install MongoDB Community Edition on your system and use the mongo shell to create a new database named 'foodieApp'.

-> use foodieApp


2. Inside the 'foodieApp' database, create a collection named 'restaurants' and insert a document for a Zomato-style restaurant with fields: name, cuisine, rating, and location.

-> db.restaurants.insertOne({
    name: "The Food House",
    cuisine: "Indian",
    rating: 4.5,
    location: {
        city: "Rajkot",
        area: "Kalawad Road"
    }
})


3. Insert three more restaurant documents into the 'restaurants' collection, making sure to use at least one field with an array data type (for example, a 'menu' array listing popular dishes).

-> db.restaurants.insertMany([
    {
        name: "Spice Villa",
        cuisine: "North Indian",
        rating: 4.2,
        location: {
            city: "Ahmedabad",
            area: "SG Highway"
        },
        menu: ["Paneer Tikka", "Butter Naan", "Dal Makhani"]
    },
    {
        name: "Pizza Corner",
        cuisine: "Italian",
        rating: 4.6,
        location: {
            city: "Rajkot",
            area: "University Road"
        },
        menu: ["Margherita Pizza", "Farmhouse Pizza", "Garlic Bread"]
    },
    {
        name: "Green Leaf",
        cuisine: "Gujarati",
        rating: 4.3,
        location: {
            city: "Surat",
            area: "Vesu"
        },
        menu: ["Gujarati Thali", "Dhokla", "Fafda"]
    }
])


4. Retrieve all documents from the 'restaurants' collection and display them in the mongo shell. Identify which BSON types are used for each field.

-> db.restaurants.find()
-> BSON Type used

        Field                 BSON Type
    1.   _id                   ObjectId
    2.   name                  String
    3.   cuisine               String
    4.   rating                Double/Number
    5.   location              Object
    6.   menu                  Array